esp_timg: three divide-by-zero crashes reachable from a guest register write.

`hw/timer/esp_timg.c` backs the C3/S3 timer groups (the classic ESP32 has
its own `esp32_timg.c`, which is why only the newer machines were hit).
Three of its divisors come straight from guest-written register fields with
no guard, and each one takes the whole emulator down with SIGFPE rather
than misbehaving:

  * `esp_t0_config_update()` divides by TIMG_Tx_DIVIDER.  A DIVIDER field of
    0 means 65536 on silicon (TRM) and is the reset value, so ANY guest that
    read-modify-writes T0CONFIG before programming its own prescaler crashes
    QEMU.  esp-hal does exactly that while `esp_rtos::start` brings up the
    scheduler tick -- the S3 guest died there, a few instructions after its
    first "Main task created" line, every time.
  * `esp_timg_rtc_cali_update()` indexes a three-entry frequency table with
    the 2-bit CLK_SEL field, so selection 3 reads past the array and can
    divide by whatever is next in memory (zero, here).
  * `esp_virtual_counter_update()` / `_alarm_in_ticks()` divide by a counter
    frequency that the two above can legitimately leave at zero.

Guard all three the way the WDT prescaler in the same file already does:
DIVIDER 0 is 65536, an out-of-range CLK_SEL falls back to the slow RC
oscillator, and a zero-frequency counter simply never ticks.

Harness-side per CLAUDE.md's QEMU isolation rule -- the guest image is
byte-identical to what ships.

